## 1. Behaviour: the comparison table takes `producerRecipesFor`'s order

`candidateRowsFor` sources `producerRecipesFor` and uses its order DIRECTLY —
no comparator, no ordering-preservation shim (Axis 2 (d)). Safe only because
#116 (`b3ed867`) shipped an explicit `(alt)` marker, so alternate status is no
longer encoded by position.

**PASSING with the code present:**

```
$ npx vitest run src/ui/chain-builder-adapter.test.ts -t "compares rubber"
 ✓ S21 P1 — producerRecipesFor is the comparison enumerator >
   compares rubber in producerRecipesFor's order — the ALTERNATE in the MIDDLE
```

**BREAK** — restore the retired grouping comparator on the comparison path, in
`src/ui/chain-builder-adapter.ts`, `candidateRowsFor`:

```ts
-  const candidates = producerRecipesFor(catalog, itemId);
+  const candidates = producerRecipesFor(catalog, itemId).sort((a, b) => {
+    if (a.isAlternate !== b.isAlternate) return a.isAlternate ? 1 : -1;
+    return a.id < b.id ? -1 : a.id > b.id ? 1 : 0;
+  });
```

Compiles: `npx tsc -b` → exit 0.

**GENUINE FAIL:**

```
 × compares rubber in producerRecipesFor's order — the ALTERNATE in the MIDDLE
 FAIL src/ui/chain-builder-adapter.test.ts > S21 P1 — producerRecipesFor is
      the comparison enumerator > compares rubber in producerRecipesFor's
      order — the ALTERNATE in the MIDDLE
 AssertionError: expected [ 'residual_rubber', 'rubber', …(1) ]
                 to deeply equal [ 'residual_rubber', …(2) ]
 Tests  1 failed | 94 passed (95)
```

Exactly ONE test dies, and it is the intended one. `rubber` is the only item in
the whole catalog whose alternate lands in a NON-TERMINAL position, which the
grouping comparator can never emit — that is what makes this row a real
revert-detector rather than a restatement.

**RESTORE + RE-RUN GREEN:** reverted; `git status --short` empty; suite green
(see §6).

