## 3. Behaviour: the converter/packager row asserts POSITIVELY

Spec item 6 calls this an upgrade: the retired `toEqual([])` was satisfied by a
filter that dropped EVERYTHING, so it could not distinguish exclusion from
over-exclusion.

**PASSING with the code present:**

```
$ npx vitest run src/ui/chain-builder-adapter.test.ts \
    -t "excludes converter/packager"
 ✓ alt-compare — candidate enumeration >
   excludes converter/packager recipes from candidacy
```

**BREAK** — an enumeration filter that drops the surviving non-alternate, in
`producerRecipesFor`:

```ts
-    (r) => r.primaryOutputId === itemId && !excluded.has(r.machineId),
+    (r) =>
+      r.primaryOutputId === itemId &&
+      !excluded.has(r.machineId) &&
+      r.isAlternate,
```

Compiles: `npx tsc -b` → exit 0.

**GENUINE FAIL:**

```
 FAIL  src/ui/chain-builder-adapter.test.ts > alt-compare — candidate
       enumeration > excludes converter/packager recipes from candidacy
 AssertionError: expected [] to deeply equal [ 'r_std' ]
```

**The upgrade, demonstrated by the measured value.** The mutant's actual output
is `[]` — which is byte-for-byte what the RETIRED expectation (`toEqual([])`)
asserted. So this mutant would have PASSED the pre-change row and FAILS the
post-change one. The spec's claim about this row is confirmed against a real
measurement, not argued.

(This mutant also reddens 29 other rows across the adapter suite, as any
enumerator-wide break must; the isolated `-t` run above is what attributes the
kill to this pin specifically.)

**RESTORE + RE-RUN GREEN:** reverted; tree clean; suite green.

